  "answer": "Lighthouses are tall structures, usually located on the coast, that emit light from a system of lamps and lenses to serve as a navigational aid for maritime navigation at sea. They are typically built in prominent locations to ensure their light is visible over long distances, often from several nautical miles away. Modern lighthouses are largely automated and may be equipped with radar transponders and radio beacons to assist in navigation and collision avoidance.",
